1)Dependency arrows in the original design:
EnrollmentService ───► CsvStudentStore
EnrollmentService ───► EmailSender
The arrow goes from the high-level policy (EnrollmentService) to the low-level details.

2)The two added abstractions should live near EnrollmentService,
because the abstractions should be defined from the needs of the high-level policy,
not the low-level CSV/e-mail details

6)DIP is the design principle saying high-level modules should depend
on abstractions rather than concrete low-level classes
DI is the technique of providing those dependencies from outside the class.
In question 3, we used dependency injection because dependencies are passed through the constructor
In question 4, we used dependency inversion because concrete details implement abstractions required by EnrollmentService